// clean-browser — the host contract.
//
// HCV-02 (Platform 16 §16.5): this file is the authoritative declaration of
// what this host provides. There is no parallel manifest. The framework
// fetches it for its Moment 1 check, `cln check` uses it at Moment 2, and the
// browser host compares a guest's embedded WIT against it at Moment 3 before
// instantiating.
//
// HCV-06 (§16.14): CI verifies that every interface declared here is really
// registered by this host, and that nothing registered is undeclared. A no-op
// or throwing stub under a declared name is a hard failure — the correct
// response to an unimplemented interface is to omit it from this file.
//
// SCOPE: nothing. `world browser` is deliberately EMPTY.
//
// This is not a placeholder awaiting content; it is the correct contract for
// the browser host as it exists today. No browser host has shipped, so under
// HCV-06 there is no interface this file may declare. An empty world is the
// one honest thing it can say.
//
// The file exists anyway because absence and emptiness are different failures.
// `OFFICIAL_HOSTS` maps clean-browser to this path, so while the file was
// missing every browser build failed at fetch — a network-shaped error for
// what is really a "this host provides nothing yet" condition. With the file
// present, a guest that imports nothing builds and runs; a guest that imports
// something fails at Moment 1 naming the interface it wanted. That is the
// diagnostic the developer needs.
//
// It also unblocks composition. `clean-mobile-surface` includes this world to
// inherit the browser surface rather than forking it (see that host's file,
// §2). Including an empty world composes exactly as well as including a full
// one, and every interface added here later is inherited by mobile for free —
// which is the whole reason mobile was written to include rather than restate.
//
// `wasi:*` is provided to every composed component by clean-host-core (CH-03)
// and is not restated here — host.wit declares the `clean:*` surface this host
// owns.

package clean:host@0.1.0;

/// The browser world.
///
/// Empty by HCV-06, not by omission. Adding the first interface here means a
/// browser host genuinely registers it.
world browser {
}
